//! Error: a `@shader` fn's body is ordinary CPU-executable Lua -- the authoring
//!        contract promises shader fns "compile
//!        to Lua as normal fns, so shader helper math is CPU-callable and
//!        `@test`-able", and nothing rejects a CPU call to one. So the `nobox`
//!        guarantee applies to it like any other body: an implicit
//!        materialization inside a `@shader` fn must reject.
//! Tags: nobox, shader, gpu, guarantee, emit-time
//!
//! The materialization below is unrelated to any GPU intrinsic: a box is
//! authorized only AT a `@gpu_only` call boundary, never anywhere inside a body
//! that happens to be annotated.
//!
//! The sink has to be a shape with nowhere to put the value but a box. A bare
//! nobox global stores LEAVES and a value-struct outer stores flat leaf keys, so
//! either would pass here for the wrong reason.
//!
//! Interpolating a WHOLE tuple that carries one is the sink, and it is chosen
//! because it is PERMANENT: asking for a single runtime-typed value is F1
//! erasure, a justified box that is meant to stay a rejection
//! (`nobox_container_gaps.rock.err`). A tuple element in a COLLECTION used to
//! stand here and was the wrong choice -- that was an open gap, it closed, and
//! this case went green while the shader rule it guards was untouched. Do not
//! re-point this at a sink the matrix lists as live.

//=== TEST CASE: nobox_materialization_in_a_shader_body ===
nobox value NV2 { x: float, y: float }

@shader(fragment)
fn leaky_frag(tc: NV2) -> Vec4 {
    let t = (tc, 1.0)
    let s = "{t}"
    return v(0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0)
}

fn main() {
    let _ = leaky_frag(NV2 { x: 1.0, y: 2.0 })
}
